WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:08.000
When living in Korea, you may find yourself
hesitating in front of the waste bins.

00:08.000 --> 00:16.000
Incorrectly mixed waste makes recycling difficult,
and used batteries also increase the risk of fire.

00:16.000 --> 00:24.000
Remember four steps:
empty, rinse, separate, and do not mix.

00:24.000 --> 00:31.000
First, completely empty anything
left inside the container.

00:31.000 --> 00:38.000
Second, rinse away food residue
and other contaminants with water.

00:38.000 --> 00:45.000
Third, remove parts made of different materials,
such as labels and caps.

00:45.000 --> 00:52.000
Fourth, do not mix different types
of recyclables together.

00:52.000 --> 01:02.000
For clear PET bottles, remove the label and contents, rinse,
compress the bottle, replace the cap, and dispose of it properly.

01:02.000 --> 01:11.000
Wash contaminated plastic, such as delivery containers, thoroughly.
If it cannot be cleaned, do not mix it with recyclables.

01:11.000 --> 01:21.000
For food waste, reduce moisture and remove contaminants,
then use local designated bags or RFID collection bins.

01:21.000 --> 01:30.000
Chicken bones and seashells are not food waste.
Put them in standard pay-as-you-throw garbage bags.

01:30.000 --> 01:42.000
Put used batteries in battery collection bins, unbroken fluorescent lamps
in lamp collection bins, and small appliances at designated collection points.

01:42.000 --> 01:51.000
Sorting recyclables this cleanly increases the chance
that they can be used again as resources.

01:51.000 --> 02:00.000
Detailed rules may vary by area.
Check K-SORT and start sorting correctly today.
